From: Canaan K230 Linux SDK
Subject: [PATCH] live555: size the RTP socket buffers for one I-frame

Carried over from the rt-smart tree's vendored live555 copy
(canmv_k230_dpu/src/rtsmart/mpp/middleware/src/live555).

Unlike 0003 this is tuning, not a bug fix, and it is a separate patch so it
can be dropped on its own. live555 asks the kernel for 50kB of RTP socket
buffer in each direction. One H.264/H.265 I-frame out of this SoC's encoder
is bigger than that, and it goes onto the wire as a single burst, so the
kernel drops the tail of every I-frame: the picture smears once per GOP.

Both directions now ask for 1MB. "increaseReceiveBufferTo()" and
"increaseSendBufferTo()" probe downwards until the kernel accepts a value,
so on a system whose net.core.rmem_max / wmem_max is lower this simply gets
whatever the limit allows rather than failing.

Not carried over from rt-smart, because nothing we build uses them:

  * StreamParser.cpp BANK_SIZE 150000 -> 512kB.  Only the file-reading
    framers go through StreamParser; librtsp_server feeds discrete framers
    from memory.
  * mediaServer/DynamicRTSPServer.cpp OutPacketBuffer::maxSize 100000 -> 1MB.
    That is the stand-alone live555MediaServer demo; librtsp_server sets
    OutPacketBuffer::maxSize itself.

diff -urN /tmp/m_a/liveMedia/MultiFramedRTPSource.cpp /tmp/m_b/liveMedia/MultiFramedRTPSource.cpp
--- a/liveMedia/MultiFramedRTPSource.cpp	2026-11-21 08:07:08.737436617 +0800
+++ b/liveMedia/MultiFramedRTPSource.cpp	2026-11-21 08:07:25.753733214 +0800
@@ -73,7 +73,13 @@
   fReorderingBuffer = new ReorderingPacketBuffer(packetFactory);
 
   // Try to use a big receive buffer for RTP:
-  increaseReceiveBufferTo(env, RTPgs->socketNum(), 50*1024);
+  // Local patch; reapply when upgrading live555.
+  // 50kB is less than a single H.264/H.265 I-frame at the bitrates this SoC
+  // encodes at, and an I-frame arrives as one burst of UDP packets. Whatever
+  // does not fit is dropped by the kernel before we ever get to read it, which
+  // shows up as a smeared picture once per GOP. Linux clamps the request to
+  // net.core.rmem_max, so asking for more than the system allows is harmless.
+  increaseReceiveBufferTo(env, RTPgs->socketNum(), 1024*1024);
 }
 
 void MultiFramedRTPSource::reset() {
diff -urN /tmp/m_a/liveMedia/RTPInterface.cpp /tmp/m_b/liveMedia/RTPInterface.cpp
--- a/liveMedia/RTPInterface.cpp	2026-11-21 08:07:08.737436617 +0800
+++ b/liveMedia/RTPInterface.cpp	2026-11-21 08:07:25.753733214 +0800
@@ -143,7 +143,9 @@
   // even if the socket was previously reported (e.g., by "select()") as having data available.
   // (This can supposedly happen if the UDP checksum fails, for example.)
   makeSocketNonBlocking(fGS->socketNum());
-  increaseSendBufferTo(envir(), fGS->socketNum(), 50*1024);
+  // Local patch; reapply when upgrading live555. See MultiFramedRTPSource.cpp
+  // for why 50kB is too small for one I-frame; the same applies on the way out.
+  increaseSendBufferTo(envir(), fGS->socketNum(), 1024*1024);
 }
 
 RTPInterface::~RTPInterface() {
